1
00:00:16,679 --> 00:00:25,004
[SPEAKER_00]: I wanted to speak about this sutta in the Samyutta Nikaya 39.16.

2
00:00:26,485 --> 00:00:31,188
[SPEAKER_00]: And it's with Venerable Sariputta.

3
00:00:31,889 --> 00:00:33,610
[SPEAKER_00]: He's been asked a question.

4
00:00:34,951 --> 00:00:35,771
[SPEAKER_00]: It's a short sutta.

5
00:00:35,791 --> 00:00:36,732
[SPEAKER_00]: I'll read it out quickly.

6
00:00:39,514 --> 00:00:44,977
[SPEAKER_00]: Venerable Sariputta, in this teaching and training, in this dhamma and discipline,

7
00:00:45,788 --> 00:00:47,490
[SPEAKER_00]: What is hard to do?

8
00:00:49,011 --> 00:00:55,738
[SPEAKER_00]: And he answers, the going forth is difficult to do in this dhamma and discipline.

9
00:00:57,639 --> 00:01:00,882
But what's hard to do for someone who has gone forth?

10
00:01:02,464 --> 00:01:05,387
When you've gone forth, it's difficult to be satisfied.

11
00:01:06,988 --> 00:01:07,669
Contented.

12
00:01:08,109 --> 00:01:10,832
Oh, yeah, it's difficult to be contented.

13
00:01:12,279 --> 00:01:16,020
But what's hard, what's difficult to do for someone who's contented?

14
00:01:17,580 --> 00:01:21,021
When you're content, it's hard to practice in line with the teaching.

15
00:01:22,701 --> 00:01:32,023
[SPEAKER_00]: And then you ask, but if a renunciant practices in line with the teaching, will it take them long to become a perfected one?

16
00:01:32,863 --> 00:01:35,204
[SPEAKER_00]: And he replies, it won't take them long.

17
00:01:38,984 --> 00:01:41,345
[SPEAKER_00]: I thought that was very nice, you know, it's quite simple.

18
00:01:43,438 --> 00:01:47,140
[SPEAKER_00]: It's kind of, well, just... just go forth.

19
00:01:48,360 --> 00:01:49,661
[SPEAKER_00]: You know, learn to be content.

20
00:01:50,461 --> 00:01:50,661
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

21
00:01:50,781 --> 00:01:51,362
[SPEAKER_00]: And practice.

22
00:01:52,002 --> 00:01:54,563
[SPEAKER_00]: And in no long time, you will have the result.

23
00:01:56,083 --> 00:01:56,303
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes.

24
00:01:56,644 --> 00:01:56,844
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

25
00:01:57,804 --> 00:02:01,465
[SPEAKER_01]: So the... yeah, exactly, the contentment needs to be learned.

26
00:02:03,546 --> 00:02:10,589
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, you have to learn how to be contented because, you know, you won't be able to sustain

27
00:02:12,030 --> 00:02:16,252
[SPEAKER_01]: Practicing in line with the dhamma on the same level all the time.

28
00:02:16,792 --> 00:02:18,353
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you're gonna have ups and downs.

29
00:02:19,753 --> 00:02:28,537
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the contentment is important, for when you have your downs, you don't go lower than being contented within the homeless life.

30
00:02:29,637 --> 00:02:34,379
[SPEAKER_01]: So, then you can easily and quickly carry on practicing when it picks up.

31
00:02:35,919 --> 00:02:37,080
[SPEAKER_01]: If you are not contented,

32
00:02:39,396 --> 00:02:50,519
[SPEAKER_01]: With the life of someone who has gone forth, then, when you experience the downs, the mind will want to go back to, like, you know, household life, basically.

33
00:02:50,679 --> 00:02:53,019
[SPEAKER_01]: So that's why it's important to be contented.

34
00:02:54,419 --> 00:03:05,962
[SPEAKER_01]: Whether your practice is going good or not, whether you're inspired or not, contentment is a kind of a basis that will sort of sustain that state of having gone forth.

35
00:03:07,038 --> 00:03:15,084
[SPEAKER_01]: Which, of course, that means at any given time, the possibility of picking up the practice along the right lines remains there for you.

36
00:03:15,425 --> 00:03:20,028
[SPEAKER_01]: You haven't excluded yourself from it, to say it like that.

37
00:03:23,111 --> 00:03:32,178
[SPEAKER_00]: What comes to mind is a person who goes forth and starts the training of virtue and sensuous strength.

38
00:03:33,418 --> 00:03:35,300
[SPEAKER_00]: It is difficult, but then

39
00:03:36,125 --> 00:03:38,607
Yeah, so he... it's difficult to sustain.

40
00:03:39,868 --> 00:03:50,557
But then, having sustained it, you know, you can become accomplished in virtue, accomplished in sentient state, and feel the joy on account of that accomplishment, you know.

41
00:03:50,957 --> 00:03:51,197
Yeah.

42
00:03:51,518 --> 00:03:58,223
So... and now you always have that joy to... to sustain you, to fall back on.

43
00:03:58,243 --> 00:04:00,885
[SPEAKER_00]: You can't fall... you don't fall below that.

44
00:04:01,446 --> 00:04:02,347
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, and that's...

45
00:04:02,367 --> 00:04:05,229
[SPEAKER_01]: I think that's exactly... yeah, that's... those are the suttas where the Buddha said,

46
00:04:05,717 --> 00:04:12,942
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, you should replace the joys of a household life with the joys of a renunciate life.

47
00:04:13,662 --> 00:04:19,486
[SPEAKER_01]: You should replace the grief and sadness of a household life with the grief and sadness of a renunciate life.

48
00:04:21,027 --> 00:04:29,612
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you're still gonna have ups and downs, but if you're contented with that life, means you have upgraded, at least in that sense.

49
00:04:30,563 --> 00:04:45,048
[SPEAKER_01]: So, because joys and griefs of a household life are much worse, much more pressuring, entangling, proliferated, and so on, than those of a renunciate life.

50
00:04:45,568 --> 00:04:56,132
[SPEAKER_01]: There are still joys ups and downs, but they're purer, to say that way, because you are withdrawn from that, you know, household life is crowded and dusty, as they say, so...

51
00:04:58,686 --> 00:05:01,168
[SPEAKER_00]: And the life gone forth is open air.

52
00:05:01,428 --> 00:05:01,688
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

53
00:05:01,788 --> 00:05:02,108
[SPEAKER_00]: Open.

54
00:05:02,709 --> 00:05:03,289
[SPEAKER_01]: It's fresh.

55
00:05:03,769 --> 00:05:04,169
[SPEAKER_01]: Freer.

56
00:05:04,189 --> 00:05:05,030
[SPEAKER_00]: It's clean.

57
00:05:07,451 --> 00:05:12,054
[SPEAKER_00]: But... and I like what is, you know, what is... it's difficult to go forth.

58
00:05:13,014 --> 00:05:13,354
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

59
00:05:13,394 --> 00:05:13,695
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

60
00:05:13,895 --> 00:05:14,775
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

61
00:05:14,935 --> 00:05:24,261
[SPEAKER_01]: It's difficult to give up that which is familiar to you, that which which you know, that which you're comfortable in.

62
00:05:24,901 --> 00:05:25,741
[SPEAKER_01]: Let's say it like that.

63
00:05:25,841 --> 00:05:26,382
[SPEAKER_01]: So...

64
00:05:27,716 --> 00:05:29,356
[SPEAKER_01]: Change is unpleasant, yeah.

65
00:05:29,496 --> 00:05:41,299
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's difficult to undergo such a change of giving up a household life for something that you are uncertain about and something that will require you to learn how to be contented with.

66
00:05:42,480 --> 00:05:45,560
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's why it's difficult to give it up, yeah.

67
00:05:45,820 --> 00:05:52,162
[SPEAKER_01]: And anybody who is trying to give it up realizes that it's difficult to give it up.

68
00:05:52,182 --> 00:05:55,423
[SPEAKER_00]: But giving up, giving it up and going forward,

69
00:06:00,143 --> 00:06:06,388
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm keeping the precepts, virtue, you know, for taking refuge in the Buddha for the rest of your life, so to speak.

70
00:06:06,889 --> 00:06:08,971
[SPEAKER_00]: You know, I will maintain this virtue.

71
00:06:09,071 --> 00:06:10,612
[SPEAKER_00]: I will maintain the central strength.

72
00:06:11,793 --> 00:06:16,257
[SPEAKER_00]: It says, you know, the Buddha says, you know, if you do that, you will experience a joy.

73
00:06:16,717 --> 00:06:17,918
[SPEAKER_00]: A joy of being blameless.

74
00:06:18,098 --> 00:06:19,700
A joy of being unpolluted.

75
00:06:20,140 --> 00:06:20,861
Yeah, eventually.

76
00:06:20,881 --> 00:06:22,802
Joy of... eventually.

77
00:06:23,043 --> 00:06:26,245
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

78
00:06:27,386 --> 00:06:28,027
[SPEAKER_00]: Which, you know,

79
00:06:28,617 --> 00:06:36,142
[SPEAKER_00]: Once you, once you have that, it's, eh... then it's easier.

80
00:06:38,644 --> 00:06:38,944
[SPEAKER_01]: What is?

81
00:06:40,485 --> 00:06:41,166
For the practice.

82
00:06:42,386 --> 00:06:43,707
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, yeah, yeah, of course.

83
00:06:43,847 --> 00:06:56,196
[SPEAKER_01]: I mean, in Manisutta, it's said that, you know, joy arises on account of unbroken virtue, contentment, contented and joyous mind gets composed.

84
00:06:56,296 --> 00:06:57,837
[SPEAKER_01]: So samādhi is born out of it.

85
00:06:59,992 --> 00:07:02,273
[SPEAKER_01]: So, yeah, it's like a necessary basis.

86
00:07:02,513 --> 00:07:03,814
[SPEAKER_01]: It's part of the practice, really.

87
00:07:05,435 --> 00:07:13,982
[SPEAKER_01]: Learning how to be content with a homeless life, it's already, in a way, practicing along the lines of the dhamma.

88
00:07:16,063 --> 00:07:20,746
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

89
00:07:21,387 --> 00:07:28,752
[SPEAKER_00]: In terms of, you know, you read that you will become happy and feel a blameless joy if you

90
00:07:29,452 --> 00:07:30,633
Maintain your virtue.

91
00:07:32,514 --> 00:07:35,537
And which is, you know, you'll feel a blameless joy.

92
00:07:38,599 --> 00:07:40,901
So, and you're not really doing anything.

93
00:07:41,862 --> 00:07:43,983
You're just not doing wrong things.

94
00:07:44,604 --> 00:07:46,245
And that joy is accessible to you.

95
00:07:46,945 --> 00:07:54,251
Just reflecting on your sīla, sīla nusati, that joy is, you know, experienced.

96
00:07:54,832 --> 00:07:55,072
Yeah.

97
00:07:55,712 --> 00:07:56,613
You know, you know, there's

98
00:07:57,123 --> 00:08:02,788
I was once dirty, now I'm quite clean.

99
00:08:03,949 --> 00:08:12,556
[SPEAKER_00]: But it's funny how just that first step, you know, the Buddha says, keep this and you'll be happy.

100
00:08:13,096 --> 00:08:14,878
[SPEAKER_00]: You know, you'll feel this blameless joy.

101
00:08:15,218 --> 00:08:26,387
That first step is kind of... not many people have faith in just that first step, which is... it's so accessible, actually.

102
00:08:26,745 --> 00:08:27,686
It's so close.

103
00:08:28,086 --> 00:08:33,429
That very lofty pleasure is so close, you know, it's accessible.

104
00:08:33,769 --> 00:08:37,292
Yet, it's like, I don't have faith in that.

105
00:08:37,392 --> 00:08:41,334
I'd rather, I'd rather do something else.

106
00:08:41,374 --> 00:08:43,135
I'd rather do meditation.

107
00:08:44,136 --> 00:08:45,997
The satipaṭṭhāna instead.

108
00:08:47,778 --> 00:08:50,200
But that's not the first step.

109
00:08:50,220 --> 00:08:52,681
So, do you have faith in the Buddha?

110
00:08:53,261 --> 00:08:54,022
Do you have faith in

111
00:08:54,963 --> 00:09:05,268
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, that's that kind of lack of clarity that sort of exists in many people.

112
00:09:05,328 --> 00:09:21,135
[SPEAKER_01]: Even if they have genuine intention to practice, they don't realize that, you know, your views of what the practice is, what's important for the practice, are views affected with ignorance.

113
00:09:21,736 --> 00:09:21,996
[SPEAKER_01]: So,

114
00:09:23,417 --> 00:09:32,443
[SPEAKER_01]: Your practice, actually, should start by questioning those views, questioning your motivation, uncovering and revealing.

115
00:09:32,823 --> 00:09:39,668
[SPEAKER_01]: Your practice shouldn't start with just taking for granted the views that you have about a practice and practicing on account of them.

116
00:09:42,409 --> 00:09:47,793
[SPEAKER_01]: And many people fall into that trap in a sense of they get the idea of what the practice is,

117
00:09:48,742 --> 00:09:57,247
[SPEAKER_01]: They, eh... obviously, you know, you develop standpoints of what's important, what's not, and then you just... they just take it for granted that that's what the practice is, that's what the Buddha was teaching.

118
00:09:57,947 --> 00:10:11,415
[SPEAKER_01]: So, they can profess, you know, that they're follower of the of the Buddha's teaching and the dhamma and so on, yet, as you say, exactly, the very first thing that the Buddha says you should do, they kind of overlooked.

119
00:10:13,341 --> 00:10:19,903
[SPEAKER_01]: It's quite easily done, when your standpoints are already far away from what's really important.

120
00:10:20,603 --> 00:10:23,864
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's why, like the Buddha said, the most blameworthy thing is the wrong view.

121
00:10:25,944 --> 00:10:31,186
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the reason for everything unwholesome, in a way.

122
00:10:31,746 --> 00:10:40,708
[SPEAKER_01]: Because if a person were to learn, or just start actually questioning themselves rightly, in a sense of, hold on, yes, I have faith that the Buddha was enlightened,

123
00:10:41,674 --> 00:10:45,157
[SPEAKER_01]: I have faith that the dhamma is well expounded.

124
00:10:45,857 --> 00:10:51,901
[SPEAKER_01]: I have faith that if I follow that dhamma, I can also, you know, reach some stage of enlightenment, free myself from suffering.

125
00:10:53,202 --> 00:11:00,667
[SPEAKER_01]: So, am I actually following clearly and understanding what the Buddha said and practicing on account of it?

126
00:11:01,488 --> 00:11:08,253
[SPEAKER_01]: Or is my idea of, you know, I'm a Buddha follower, kind of, and I have faith in dhamma abstractly, but practically I'm doing something else.

127
00:11:08,613 --> 00:11:09,914
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, what do I put first?

128
00:11:10,805 --> 00:11:13,767
[SPEAKER_01]: Am I doing my meditation for the sake of enlightenment?

129
00:11:13,847 --> 00:11:22,432
[SPEAKER_01]: Or have I really developed the unbroken, unblemished aggregate of virtue, whereby I just recollect it and I experience pleasure on account of it?

130
00:11:22,553 --> 00:11:25,334
[SPEAKER_01]: Because that's what the Buddha said you should do.

131
00:11:25,474 --> 00:11:29,537
[SPEAKER_00]: And you shouldn't be doing that meditation before you've done that.

132
00:11:29,697 --> 00:11:30,558
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, exactly.

133
00:11:30,638 --> 00:11:35,201
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, if you start questioning as to see if you're actually doing what the Buddha said you should do,

134
00:11:36,806 --> 00:11:40,388
[SPEAKER_01]: Not doing what you think the Buddha said you should do.

135
00:11:40,589 --> 00:11:41,689
[SPEAKER_01]: So, make the effort.

136
00:11:41,790 --> 00:11:46,934
[SPEAKER_01]: Or, if you are absolutely convinced, then, again, it will do you no harm to re-evaluate it.

137
00:11:47,394 --> 00:11:49,255
[SPEAKER_01]: And, if you arrive at the same answers, great.

138
00:11:49,976 --> 00:11:51,577
[SPEAKER_01]: But, you have to be honest with yourself.

139
00:11:51,617 --> 00:11:55,540
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you are, you would see that, well, there are certain things that the Buddha said you should do.

140
00:11:55,621 --> 00:11:57,302
[SPEAKER_01]: And, have I done those things?

141
00:11:57,482 --> 00:12:04,708
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, yes, something as simple as developing virtue, well, the Buddha said, if you actually develop it rightly, yes,

142
00:12:06,118 --> 00:12:11,201
[SPEAKER_01]: Simply recollecting that, recollecting the Buddha and the Saṅgha, it will it will fill me with joy and samādhi.

143
00:12:12,502 --> 00:12:13,223
[SPEAKER_01]: Is that the case?

144
00:12:13,963 --> 00:12:16,344
[SPEAKER_01]: When I recollect my virtue, am I joyous?

145
00:12:16,424 --> 00:12:17,225
[SPEAKER_01]: Am I contented?

146
00:12:17,625 --> 00:12:22,328
[SPEAKER_01]: Am I sort of withdrawn from unwholesome states simply on account of that recollection?

147
00:12:23,188 --> 00:12:29,992
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, if the answer is no, then your idea of virtue and your practice of virtue is obviously not fulfilled.

148
00:12:30,052 --> 00:12:34,155
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not fulfilled from the Buddhist standpoint of view, from the point of view of practicing the dhamma.

149
00:12:35,095 --> 00:12:42,897
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you could ignore it and carry on thinking it's fine, or you could just say to yourself, hold on, why don't I fulfill this?

150
00:12:43,038 --> 00:12:50,880
[SPEAKER_01]: It is something very straightforward, hard to misunderstand, easy to overlook, but hard to misunderstand if you look at it.

151
00:12:51,480 --> 00:12:52,320
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not complex.

152
00:12:53,261 --> 00:12:56,842
[SPEAKER_01]: The reason people don't do it is not because it's just, oh, I don't understand what I'm supposed to do.

153
00:12:56,882 --> 00:12:57,942
[SPEAKER_01]: No, it's because they overlook it.

154
00:12:58,842 --> 00:13:03,584
[SPEAKER_01]: They just drift and do something else, or do something on top of it, instead of it.

155
00:13:04,380 --> 00:13:06,681
[SPEAKER_01]: Already at the expense of it.

156
00:13:06,841 --> 00:13:08,261
[SPEAKER_01]: So that's kind of the point.

157
00:13:08,381 --> 00:13:10,222
[SPEAKER_01]: That honesty and self-reflection.

158
00:13:10,422 --> 00:13:13,663
[SPEAKER_01]: And then you would re-evaluate things you think you know.

159
00:13:14,003 --> 00:13:19,405
[SPEAKER_01]: And again, by reading the suttas, you would see quite clearly what the first thing to do is.

160
00:13:20,145 --> 00:13:22,746
[SPEAKER_01]: And as we said in the opening, that you realize what that is already.

161
00:13:23,366 --> 00:13:24,827
[SPEAKER_01]: By developing your virtue,

162
00:13:26,136 --> 00:13:31,104
[SPEAKER_01]: Contentment, on account of the unblemished aggregate of virtue, you are practicing in line with the dhamma.

163
00:13:31,184 --> 00:13:33,227
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's not like, oh, I'll do that and then I'll do the dhamma.

164
00:13:33,288 --> 00:13:35,050
[SPEAKER_01]: That is already doing the dhamma rightly.

165
00:13:35,690 --> 00:13:58,335
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, as the Buddha said, even within the five precepts, somebody keeping the five precepts, if they do it in that responsible sense, purified until it's... until the aggregate of that virtue is developed, until it's like, oh, I have been keeping the five precepts, have not intentionally went against them for a long time, the recognition of that will arise.

166
00:13:59,190 --> 00:14:11,777
[SPEAKER_01]: And the Buddha said, so even within those five precepts, if a person then recollects that unblemished five precepts and virtue, recollects the Buddha, the dhamma and the saṅgha, that is their unification of mind.

167
00:14:11,917 --> 00:14:13,038
[SPEAKER_01]: That is their samādhi.

168
00:14:13,398 --> 00:14:16,580
[SPEAKER_01]: A factual samādhi arises as a result of it.

169
00:14:18,721 --> 00:14:23,024
[SPEAKER_00]: The aggregate of samādhi comes from that aggregate of sīla.

170
00:14:23,264 --> 00:14:24,204
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly, exactly.

171
00:14:24,464 --> 00:14:27,246
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why it's samādhi, because you're not doing it.

172
00:14:28,250 --> 00:14:32,053
[SPEAKER_01]: You're not creating it through your own sort of, you know, contrived techniques and so on.

173
00:14:32,393 --> 00:14:34,595
[SPEAKER_01]: It's the result of the aggregate of virtue.

174
00:14:34,795 --> 00:14:43,082
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's the result of your practice, which revolved around self-control and abstaining from unwholesome things, things rooted in unwholesome.

175
00:14:44,063 --> 00:14:47,325
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you sustain that long enough, that samādhi will follow.

176
00:14:47,886 --> 00:14:49,167
[SPEAKER_01]: Samādhi will be result of it.

177
00:14:49,667 --> 00:14:52,910
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, when you are composed on account of contentment,

178
00:14:53,687 --> 00:14:56,428
[SPEAKER_01]: That arose because of your unbroken virtue.

179
00:14:57,189 --> 00:14:59,490
[SPEAKER_01]: Your unbroken virtue will be purified even further.

180
00:14:59,950 --> 00:15:06,694
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, you know, being composed means not being as pressured or not being pressured as much.

181
00:15:06,794 --> 00:15:14,338
[SPEAKER_01]: As if... as when you are pressured when you're not composed and then the mind gets pulled and pressured by various things that sort of you have to resist.

182
00:15:15,959 --> 00:15:22,582
[SPEAKER_01]: So, composure that arises on account of the virtue will then feed and help the virtue and sustain the virtue further.

183
00:15:27,062 --> 00:15:32,835
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't have to lean the situation further, you know, abandoning the hindrances, you know, just...

184
00:15:33,211 --> 00:15:36,772
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, you can then start refining it and so on.

185
00:15:37,192 --> 00:15:43,234
[SPEAKER_01]: In order to refine something, first you need to get rid of the crude impurities.

186
00:15:43,274 --> 00:15:45,594
[SPEAKER_01]: I mean, there are many similes like purifying the gold.

187
00:15:46,054 --> 00:15:52,996
[SPEAKER_01]: You know, you don't just ignore all the junk around the gold dust and then start purifying the specific... No.

